      <p>That heavy, wobbly Sheesham wood chair from your grandparents' ancestral home, the solid study desk that survived three siblings' board exams, or the dark-polished bedside table sitting neglected in your storeroom. Structurally, the wood is sound and heavy—crafted decades ago from solid timber that puts modern flimsy flatpack furniture to shame. But cosmetically, the dated orange-brown spirit polish and surface scratches make it clash with your contemporary home aesthetic.</p>

